# Week 12 — Knowledge Gap Formulation for Compounding

**Name:** Zemzem Hibet
**Program:** The Tenacious Recruitment Program (TRP)
**Week:** 12 of 12
**Focus:** Inference-time mechanics, Agent and tool-use internals, Training and post-training mechanics, Evaluation and statistics

---

## What This Week Is

Weeks 0–11 were about building systems. Week 12 is about understanding them deeply enough to teach them.

Each day I was paired with a colleague. We each identified a genuine gap in our understanding of the day's AI engineering topic — grounded in our own Week 10 and 11 portfolio work. We sharpened each other's questions on a morning call, spent the day researching our partner's question, wrote a blog post and LinkedIn post explaining it, then critiqued each other's work on an evening call.

Result: 8 gaps closed, 4 blog posts, 3 LinkedIn posts, 4 concrete edits to prior portfolio work.

---

## Daily Pairs

| Day | Topic | My Question | Partner's Question |
|-----|-------|-------------|-------------------|
| 1 | Inference-time mechanics | KV cache stores keys and values at every transformer layer — why does byte-identical prefix identity matter and what does recomputation actually cost? | How to detect and quantify length bias in Qwen3-Next-80B given chosen emails averaging 108 words and rejected averaging 231 words |
| 2 | Agent and tool-use internals | Why does my Week 10 agent loop for 682 seconds — what breaks at the token level when a model fails to complete a tool call? | What happens at the token level when a model decides to call a tool vs respond in plain text |
| 3 | Training and post-training mechanics | Why does SimPO move faster than DPO on small datasets — what does removing the reference model actually do to the gradient? | How to diagnose whether LoRA preference training is learning stable behavior versus exploiting shortcuts in the judge rubric |
| 4 | Evaluation and statistics | My benchmark passes three contamination checks but I cannot explain why they don't prove the base model didn't see my tasks during pre-training | Whether a 3-failure to 0-failure improvement on 89 examples is statistically meaningful or sampling noise |

